    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The combined effect of mechanical fabric reorganization and clay diagenesis on reduction in fault permeability was assessed for a shale smear in an underground exposure. Mercury injection analyses indicate a reduction in permeability by 1-2 orders of magnitude compared to reference samples taken 2 m away from the fault. Permeability reduction in shale smear correlates with a distinct fabric reorganization which is attributed to the combination of mechanical clay-particle reorientation and diagenetic clay neoformation. An observed reduction in smectite content and a corresponding increase in kaolinite component is indicative of interaction of organic-rich shale with infiltrated meteoric fluids and suggests that water- mineral reactions are an integral component of shale smear formation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
